    "result_text": "The build passes and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `bulkSignupWarning` flag for the PetClinic owner API. The change follows the existing pattern for read-only derived fields (like `membershipTier`, `locality`) and reuses the per-day accumulation logic already used by the daily-registration-limit rule.\n\n**What I changed:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `bulkSignupWarning` boolean property to the `Owner` schema. The DTO (`OwnerDto`) is generated from this spec at build time, so this gives us the `setBulkSignupWarning(Boolean)` accessor.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added `@Mapping(target = \"bulkSignupWarning\", ignore = true)` so MapStruct leaves the field for the controller to populate (it can't be derived from a single `Owner` alone since it needs a global count).\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Added a `bulkSignupWarning(LocalDate)` helper and a `BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 80` constant. It counts owners sharing a `registrationDate` (the same accumulation path as `rejectDailyRegistrationLimit`, which uses a hard cap of 100) and returns `true` when that count exceeds 80. The flag is set on the DTO in both `addOwner` (the signup response) and `getOwner` (read-back), so the value is consistent across create and read.\n\n**Behavior:** For a fresh signup (few owners that day), the count is well under 80, so the flag is `false` \u2014 matching the acceptance test's `coreNoBulkWarningUnderThreshold`, which asserts `$.bulkSignupWarning` is `false`. The flag flips to `true` once more than 80 owners exist for the day, well below the hard 100/day limit. A `null` registration date (e.g. seed owners) is never flagged.\n\nOne design note worth flagging: I interpreted \"created today\" as \"owners sharing this owner's registration-date bucket\" so the create response and the read-back agree, and the value stays stable per owner. The only test exercises the false branch, so if you specifically want the read-back to always re-evaluate against the *current* server date instead, that's a one-line change \u2014 let me know.",
